      "page_title": "Rules, Tools, and the Checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Seven Rules",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "1. Quote every expansion by default.",
            "2. Exceptions: `[[ ]]`, `(( ))`, assignments, globs.",
            "3. Use arrays, never strings, for lists.",
            "4. `\"$@\"` to forward; `\"$*\"` only for joining.",
            "5. `-print0` / `-0` / `-d ''` for filenames.",
            "6. `printf '%s\\n'` instead of `echo`.",
            "7. Parameter expansion over forking tools."
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Script Preamble"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -euo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'",
            "# knowing set -e has holes is key"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Debugging Tools"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`shellcheck`",
              "static analysis, SC2086"
            ],
            [
              "`set -x`",
              "trace after expansion"
            ],
            [
              "`declare -p`",
              "show type and quoting"
            ],
            [
              "`printf '<%s>\\n'`",
              "count arguments"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Portability"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Arrays, `${var,,}`, `${var@Q}` are Bash-only.",
            "Absent from POSIX `sh`.",
            "`#!/bin/sh` must use `\"$@\"`, `case`, `set --`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Quote-Free Zones"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Context",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "test syntax",
              "`[[ $x == y ]]`"
            ],
            [
              "arithmetic",
              "`(( n + 1 ))`"
            ],
            [
              "plain assignment",
              "`x=$y`"
            ],
            [
              "deliberate split",
              "`arr=($s)`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Argument Dumper"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "args() { printf '<%s>\\n' \"$@\"; }",
            "args a 'b c' ''",
            "# <a>  <b c>  <>  -> 3 args"
          ],
          "note": "shows every argument boundary"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "set -e Has Holes",
          "text": "Silent inside `x=$(cmd)` and any ==tested== command. `export VAR=$(cmd)` **masks** the failure — split it."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Closing Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The One Sentence",
          "text": "The shell expands, then splits, then globs, then strips quotes. Quotes turn off splitting and globbing. Arrays preserve boundaries that a single string cannot."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "8. Final Takeaway"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "If a variable is not inside double quotes, you have promised the shell it contains no spaces, no globs, and no surprises — and filenames break that promise every day."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 3 of BASH QUOTING & EXPANSION. Every Bash script that breaks on a filename with a space breaks for the same reason: the shell expands, then splits, then globs, then strips quotes — and an unquoted variable opts into all of it. 10 pages of field notes on quoting rules, the expansion pipeline, IFS, globbing, parameter expansion, arrays, and \"$@\" vs $*. Save it before the next `rm -rf $dir/`.",
